00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.680
Now, I'm sure you're
not here to listen

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:15.620
to me talk about how awesome
engineering at Walmart is.

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.350
So let's just dive
into the actual theme

00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:21.180
of this presentation.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:25.490
So once upon a time, there was
a monolith, perhaps multiple.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.330
And of course, this monolith
was beautifully architected.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:33.020
It made our life so great and
fulfilled so many business

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:33.998
values.

00:01:33.998 --> 00:01:35.540
And of course, my
slides, by the way,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.090
are an oversimplification
of orders of magnitude.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.120
But essentially this
monolith was very modular

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.870
with various modules
inside of this thing.

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:44.328
And these modules
would essentially

00:01:44.328 --> 00:01:46.010
communicate with
each other to fulfill

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.410
whatever responsibilities
they had.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:50.450
And you know, the
invocation between modules

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.200
was some form of in-memory
invocation function calls

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.000
or method calls.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.220
And you know,
generally this actually

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.690
brought us a pretty long
way, until, of course,

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.600
a certain threshold was
breached and we woke up one day

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.020
and we realized, well,
the size of the code base,

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:12.110
the complexity of the overall
system, the scalability

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:14.390
challenges that we're
facing, and in general

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.480
the whole development velocity
is definitely not where it's

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:18.960
supposed to be anymore.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.180
And so we did the obvious thing.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.960
And at the time,
we referred to it

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.680
as introducing a
service-oriented architecture.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.020
Today we kind of
talk about it as--

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.660
or refer to it as a
microservices architecture.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.520
We broke up the monolith
and decomposed it

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.840
into individual services.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.850
So in a simplified
sense, essentially, we

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:41.780
took all of those modules and
made them individual services.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.630
And with these
individual services,

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.750
we're able to kind of develop
them individually, scale them

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:50.355
individually, and
maintain them better.

00:02:50.355 --> 00:02:52.730
Of course, we had to change
our whole engineering culture

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.110
at the company.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.920
But life was much better
now, and velocity came back.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.270
However, of course, by solving
the kind of old set of problems

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:04.770
that we had with the
monolith, now all of a sudden

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.010
we had a brand new
set of challenges

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.823
that we had to solve with
this new architecture.

00:03:09.823 --> 00:03:11.240
One of the things
is, for example,

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:15.468
the act of you know
servicing vocation is

00:03:15.468 --> 00:03:17.510
all of a sudden very
different, like these arrows

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:19.197
in between the services
become much more

00:03:19.197 --> 00:03:21.530
critical and important and
we have to very much focus on

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.310
them, whereas in a monolith,
you know, when a module invokes

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:27.710
another module,
essentially it's usually

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.210
a simple matter of
preparing a stack

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.670
and having the CPU jump to
a particular instruction.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:34.500
Now all of a sudden we need to
actually traverse the network.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.570
And this is something,
obviously, that can fill often.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.580
And so we need some
type of resiliency.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:43.010
Also, how do all of these
services discover each other?

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.130
Like, how does A know
where to find service B?

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:49.250
And if service B kind of
changes its whereabouts,

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.550
how does service A
get notified of that?

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:57.200
And how do we even observe this
overall kind of large system?

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.540
How do we know what's going
on at any point in time

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.120
with our kind of
larger infrastructure?

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.580
And debugging, how do
we even debug and trace

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.400
kind of problems?

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.870
How do we pinpoint
the particular source

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:12.378
of any problem that we're
facing in production?

00:04:12.378 --> 00:04:14.420
And of course, you know,
orchestration, you know,

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.890
now that we're kind of invoking
services via remote calls,

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:20.680
somehow we need some
type of governance

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:22.263
where everything is
done appropriately

00:04:22.263 --> 00:04:24.240
and we need some type
of policy enforcement.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.920
So we have all of
these new challenges.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.160
And again, we were
quite early to this.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.183
And the first solution to
this new set of challenges

00:04:31.183 --> 00:04:32.600
was to kind of
introduce some type

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.940
of centralized intermediary.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.800
And you can think of
an enterprise service

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.580
bus or some type of
API gateway of sorts.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.900
And the idea was pretty simple.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.420
Essentially, instead of
having all of the services

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.940
directly talk to each other
and instantiate point to point

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.520
connections, now what they
do is they kind of talk

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.040
through this centralized
intermediary.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.130
And it would take care of things
like discovery and routing

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.710
and resiliency and policy
enforcement and observability

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:58.910
and you name it.

00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:01.520
It was all there to
a certain extent.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:04.310
And that certainly
solved the problems

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:06.640
that we had when
we kind of naively

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:11.890
decomposed our monolith into
individual microservices.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:15.730
But of course, once again,
having solved the current set

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.860
of problems, we
have now introduced

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:19.480
a brand new set of problems.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.440
With this particular
architecture,

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.135
some of the most
important things

00:05:23.135 --> 00:05:24.510
is the fact that
we've introduced

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.710
a single point of failure.

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:30.250
Since everyone-- all of the
services are relying on this

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:34.900
kind of centralized broker
or intermediary of sorts

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.400
to communicate with each other--

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.040
if this thing goes
down, obviously no one

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:43.210
can communicate with each
other, and we have a problem.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:45.100
In addition, we
also introduced kind

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:47.470
of a different form of
scalability challenges,

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:51.760
because as our microservices
architecture is growing--

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:55.390
and of course at Walmart, we
went from dozens to hundreds

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:58.390
to thousands of services now--

00:05:58.390 --> 00:06:00.070
this centralized
kind of system has

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:01.570
to account for the
additional load

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:04.450
and throughput and
overall weight that

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:05.690
is put on the system.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:10.330
So we as a platform team have
to constantly maintain, monitor

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.190
this thing and kind
of keep scaling it up

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.550
as the usage of it grows.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:17.800
Of course, the latency
story isn't all that

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:20.290
great either here,
because when we decompose

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:23.680
the monolith, of course, and we
introduce remote invocations,

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.300
all of a sudden our
latencies went in memory

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:33.430
to kind of remote calls
that kind of deteriorated

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:37.298
the kind of performance
with orders of magnitude.

00:06:37.298 --> 00:06:39.340
Now all of a sudden we're
actually doing multiple

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.910
hops on a network, which is
definitely not a great thing

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:44.890
for performance overall.

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:49.180
And from a kind of
platform perspective,

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.730
as we're trying to kind
of innovate and improve

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:54.700
the overall kind of platform,
whenever we're looking

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:57.490
to kind of make changes
or add new features

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:01.240
or even fix bugs
at runtime, we have

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:02.938
to be very careful
because any change we

00:07:02.938 --> 00:07:04.480
do in this kind of
centralized system

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.970
is impacting all of the
tenants at the same time.

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.620
So that is kind of very tricky.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:14.470
And at some point, the
velocity of our improvements

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:19.720
comes to a grinding
halt. And so we obviously

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.420
recognize that the fact that
these problems that we're now

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.670
facing are due to
the fact that we

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:28.000
have this kind of
centralized nature.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:29.890
Our solution has
been centralized.

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:32.540
And that's kind of the culprit
of these particular problems.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.120
So the obvious kind
of next step is

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:37.270
to take that centralized
solution and decentralize it.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:41.110
So at the time, the thing
that we came up with

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.000
was to create a--

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.490
let's call it a
microservices framework

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:49.180
of sorts, essentially like a
framework or set of libraries,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:52.810
that take care of all
of the same concerns

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:57.070
that the centralized system was
doing, the same kind of routing

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:00.490
and discovery and resiliency,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:03.010
And you know, thereby
we would kind of

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:05.830
have this framework take care
of all of those requirements

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:10.000
and we would eliminate the need
of this centralized system.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:13.750
Now, this certainly
kind of solved

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:18.500
all of those problems we
had at that point in time.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.240
But once again, we've kind
of changed architecture

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:27.160
and we went from an old set of
problems and now all of sudden

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.400
we have a brand new set
of problems once again.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.980
The problems that we're
facing now, for example,

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:37.330
is the fact that this particular
solution is non polyglot.

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.240
Now, in the case of Walmart,
our engineering teams

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.030
are free to use whatever
technology they would like

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:46.440
to use and that they feel is the
best fit for their kind of use

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:46.940
cases.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.870
So you will find any
language, any framework,

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:53.530
any set of libraries being
utilized in our company.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.750
And we as a platform
team somehow

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:59.890
have to facilitate these
kind of platform concerns

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:02.710
for all of these
technology stacks, which,

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.410
in this particular architecture,
essentially boils down

00:09:05.410 --> 00:09:09.400
to us having to kind of port
this microservices framework

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.710
to all of the different
technology stacks, which

00:09:11.710 --> 00:09:15.110
is definitely quite the
overhead on our team.

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:20.230
In addition, the actual kind
of microservices framework

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:23.170
now is all of the sudden
hardcoded into the application.

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:30.760
So your platform is now a part
of the actual applications,

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:34.150
and thereby this
makes kind of changes

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:35.950
very difficult because
again, if we're

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:39.880
trying to innovate, improve
the platform, add new features

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:43.180
and fix new bugs, we have
to, in this architecture,

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:45.190
go through each and
every application theme

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:47.200
and essentially beg
them to kind of upgrade

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:49.690
to the latest and
greatest, which

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:53.230
also makes things extremely
challenging in practice for us.

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.720
And of course, enforcement
of appropriate usage

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.870
of this framework is
also quite tricky.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:06.570
So I suppose the question here
is, is there a middle ground?

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:10.610
Between the centralized
solution that we had

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:14.200
and the kind of more distributed
solution using frameworks

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:18.530
where we don't have the
centralized kind of problems

00:10:18.530 --> 00:10:21.020
going on, is there
something that would give us

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:22.250
the best of both worlds?

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:25.820
And without the problems
that come with these two

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:27.590
kind of architectural patterns?

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:29.570
And essentially, the
answer to the question

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:33.110
presented itself with the
introduction, or at least

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:36.720
the introduction for us,
to the sidecar pattern.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.850
So when we learned about
this particular pattern,

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.750
we were extremely excited,
because this was--

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:46.130
we immediately recognized
that this pattern

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:48.830
would be essentially
the next step in kind

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:51.950
of evolving our overall
architecture, especially

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.120
as a platform theme.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:55.950
And the idea is pretty simple.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.840
We essentially take whatever
the microservices framework was

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:04.820
doing or that centralized
kind of solution and we

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:08.100
extract that into
a sidecar proxy.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:10.910
And a sidecar proxy would
facilitate the same use cases

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:14.960
and requirements, but no
longer be actually hardcoded

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:16.820
in the application
while still maintaining

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:19.465
that decentralized
kind of characteristic.

00:11:19.465 --> 00:11:20.840
And so this solution
is beautiful

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:23.060
because it's transparent.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:23.983
It's fully polyglot.

00:11:23.983 --> 00:11:25.400
We only have to
implement it once.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.350
We don't need to port it to
all of the different technology

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:28.220
stacks.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:30.370
It's fully decentralized.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.270
And it's also efficient
because we're not

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:36.860
adding those kind of multiple
remote hops on the network.

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:39.780
So very excited to start
implementing this thing.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:41.880
And as we start
thinking about, OK,

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.090
how do we get sidecars onto
each and every workload

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.440
that we're running, the
first thing you realize

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.140
is, well, obviously
somehow we'll

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.383
have to kind of
manage and maintain

00:11:50.383 --> 00:11:52.550
the operational semantics
of each and every sidiecar

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:54.190
that we have in
our architecture.

00:11:54.190 --> 00:11:56.440
So we need to kind of be
able to dynamically configure

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:59.010
these things and
change how they behave

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:04.590
as the conditions of our
overall system are changing.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:07.690
And so obviously we need
some type of control plane

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:10.640
to manage all of this.

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.710
And so this is kind
of the architecture

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:15.440
or the architecture
idea that we landed on.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.050
And thus far, I've
only shown you

00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:21.620
kind of general patterns
and boxes and arrows.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.483
I suppose the big
question now is, OK,

00:12:23.483 --> 00:12:25.400
how do we go about
actually implementing this?

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:27.500
What are the ready
kind of solutions

00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:31.240
that we could employ to get
some architecture like this

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:32.420
to kind of work for us?

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:36.380
And so we're actually in a
very luxury type of position

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.570
because in this
day and age, there

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:40.610
are many different types
of service measures

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:45.200
that one could kind of choose
from to kind of realize

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:46.650
this particular architecture.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:50.840
Now, for us, since we were
very early to the game

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:54.600
with this particular
kind of solution,

00:12:54.600 --> 00:13:01.040
we essentially adopted
the first service mesh

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.500
that that came out, at
least that we knew of,

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:04.730
and that was Linkerd.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:08.930
So Linkerd kind of brought
about this kind of revolution

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:12.050
in thinking with regards to
handling platform concerns

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:13.610
in a sidecar.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:15.530
And we were very
quick to kind start

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:18.350
to think about how do we bring
it into our architecture, what

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:21.360
do we need to do to make
this thing a reality?

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:25.370
And so us being Walmart,
obviously we're not a startup.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:27.380
We can't just take
something that is ready

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.528
and just think it's just
going to work for us.

00:13:29.528 --> 00:13:31.070
We need to integrate
it into a larger

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:33.960
pre-existing architecture.

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.600
And so we already had systems
for service registration

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.420
and identity management,
for cloud configuration,

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:43.190
and a system for
telemetry and alerting

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:45.020
and you name it,
which was essentially

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:47.150
our kind of de
facto control plane.

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:49.220
And the beautiful
thing about Linkerd

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:51.020
is the fact that
it's quite modular

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:53.520
and we could kind of extend
it and implement plugins.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:54.470
So that's what we did.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:56.630
We implemented a whole
bunch of plugins, integrated

00:13:56.630 --> 00:14:01.100
into our existing ecosystem in
the preexisting architecture.

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:03.480
And we essentially hit
the ground running.

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:04.940
Now, the other
interesting thing is

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:08.300
that we were also able to
implement a policy engine,

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:10.820
or actually we ported the
existing policy engine

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:13.190
that we also had in
the previous solutions

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:16.130
and now had that execute
in linkerd directly

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:16.820
in the sidecar.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:20.570
So we even had a transparent
backward compatible solution

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:23.460
for this kind of next
generation architecture.

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:26.820
Now again, us being
Walmart and having

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:30.710
this kind of diverse landscape
of technologies and use cases

00:14:30.710 --> 00:14:33.590
and requirements
and immense scale,

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:36.440
there's always room
for different solutions

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:37.610
to co-exist.

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:39.980
And so at this point,
we're also thinking

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.390
about going forward
with adopting

00:14:43.390 --> 00:14:45.770
Istio as a service mesh.

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:48.187
And Istio is actually quite
beautiful, as well-- very

00:14:48.187 --> 00:14:50.270
modular, and we're doing
the same kind of exercise

00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:52.460
with integrating
it into our kind

00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:54.210
of preexisting architecture.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:57.680
And what you see
here on the picture

00:14:57.680 --> 00:14:59.907
doesn't really depict
the integrations

00:14:59.907 --> 00:15:01.490
that we've done
because I don't really

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:03.830
have enough time to kind of
go into detail about that.

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:06.320
So feel free to kind of reach
out to me after the talk.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:09.580
But essentially, you'll notice
of a pretty much default

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:13.860
setup with Istio, minus a
couple of subtle differences.

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:16.520
So for one, we're not
leveraging the egress gateway

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:17.460
at this point.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:21.080
We've kind of offloaded its
concerns onto the sidecars

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:21.830
directly.

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:24.080
And the other thing
is that we decided

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:27.620
not to use the policy mixer,
which essentially means

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:30.260
that we're not leveraging
the native policy enforcement

00:15:30.260 --> 00:15:31.653
that Istio provides.

00:15:31.653 --> 00:15:34.070
And that's for good reason,
because we have our own policy

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:34.837
engine.

00:15:34.837 --> 00:15:36.920
And we need to be backward
compatible, once again,

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:39.000
with all of this
preexisting architecture.

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:42.830
And so therefore, we essentially
started off, actually,

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:45.170
with implementing an
external mixer adapter.

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:48.230
But since our policies are
not really properly cacheable,

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:51.420
doing remote policy
enforcement is quite expensive.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:54.020
And the latency
numbers weren't really

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:55.560
where we wanted them to be.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:58.940
However, Istio and Envoy we
have this kind of awesome thing

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:01.250
where essentially there's
some type of JIT compiler

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:02.570
inside of Envoy.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:08.570
So you can kind of send
Lua scripts to Envoy,

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:11.180
and then thereby have
it kind of execute

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:13.350
any additional
semantics that you want.

00:16:13.350 --> 00:16:15.160
And so what we ended
up doing is kind

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:18.890
of porting our existing
policy engine to Lua

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:20.750
and essentially
leveraging that to get

00:16:20.750 --> 00:16:24.260
the same kind of transparent
backward compatible setup,

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:26.700
but now with this Istio.

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:29.330
So there's that.

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:31.610
So now if there's
one thing I'd like

00:16:31.610 --> 00:16:34.490
you to take away from at least
my part of this particular talk

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:37.250
is the fact that as we've
kind of been evolving

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:39.920
our architecture and making
steps into kind of introducing

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:43.790
new patterns and everything,
essentially the commonality is

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:45.710
that with every
improvement we make,

00:16:45.710 --> 00:16:49.070
we're taking an old set of
problems and solving them,

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:51.840
but thereby introducing a
brand new set of problems.

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.860
And so we're under no
illusion that the service mesh

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:57.530
is some type of silver
bullet that solves

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:59.900
all problems for ages to come.

00:16:59.900 --> 00:17:02.360
Clearly, there will
be a brand new set

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:05.849
of challenges which will take
things to the next level.

00:17:05.849 --> 00:17:07.460
Now, what are those
next set-- what

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:09.390
is that next set of challenges?

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:10.438
I have some ideas.

00:17:10.438 --> 00:17:11.480
I don't know exactly yet.

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:13.130
Perhaps I'll have
an answer next year.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.589
But we're very excited with
this kind of new evolution,

00:17:18.589 --> 00:17:21.050
especially as a platform theme.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:24.128
So with that, I will thank
you for your attention.

00:17:24.128 --> 00:17:25.920
And please give a warm
welcome to Prajakta.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:37.550
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
thank you, Georgi.

00:17:37.550 --> 00:17:39.930
And I think Georgi
said it really well.

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:42.920
We have to make sure that we
solve the challenges that you

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:46.350
face as you bring in all
of these new technologies.

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:48.660
So Georgi, let me tell you
about a set of challenges

00:17:48.660 --> 00:17:51.100
I keep hearing from
Google Cloud customers.

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:52.940
And that's, like
Georgi mentioned,

00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:55.940
a very nice pattern on how
they went to the service mesh.

00:17:55.940 --> 00:17:58.280
Similarly, we have
questions from customers

00:17:58.280 --> 00:17:59.930
that how do I go
and bring service

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:01.490
mesh into my Brownfield?

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:04.580
Or how do I go and containerize
into my existing Brownfield

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:06.890
deployment?

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:08.980
Because if you really
think of the customer,

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:10.730
they don't really care
about service mesh.

00:18:10.730 --> 00:18:12.945
They're not really
caring about containers.

00:18:12.945 --> 00:18:15.320
What they're really asking
for is infrastructure and data

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:16.580
scalability.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:19.560
They want smaller independent
development teams.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.290
They want to ship faster.

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:24.210
And they want to keep or deploy
services wherever they want.

00:18:24.210 --> 00:18:26.870
But they want to manage
all of these uniformly.

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:29.600
And they definitely don't want
to build a services management

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:31.730
infrastructure or manage it.

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:34.280
And they want to migrate
from their starting point

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:36.680
at their pace.

00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:38.780
So there are four
transformational technologies

00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:40.580
that I keep hearing
customers ask

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:43.580
on how to bring into their
Brownfield-- microservices,

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:47.540
containerization, service
mesh, and serverless.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:49.410
Have several different patterns.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:51.470
I'm going to talk about
one or two patterns

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:53.190
on how customers do this.

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:57.050
But one frequent question I
hear is, should I mesh first

00:18:57.050 --> 00:18:59.060
or should I containerize first?

00:18:59.060 --> 00:19:02.100
And I want to describe
two different approaches.

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:04.453
These are inspired by our
two real world customers

00:19:04.453 --> 00:19:05.870
on how they actually
did it or how

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:08.420
they're thinking of doing it.

00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:10.790
Let's talk about
customer A. Now,

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:15.480
their strategy is to mesh
first and then containerize.

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:16.510
Why is that?

00:19:16.510 --> 00:19:19.350
So one thing I want to actually
say before even we deep

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:21.270
dive into customer
A is that there

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:24.150
is a very common misconception
that service mesh is

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:26.040
for containerized services.

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:28.150
And actually, that's not true.

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:32.220
So let's look at our customer A.
This is a pretty big customer.

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:35.670
They started their journey to
Google Cloud a few years back.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:37.560
And they had one big monolith.

00:19:37.560 --> 00:19:38.880
And then they chopped it up.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:40.603
So it went to 20 microservices.

00:19:40.603 --> 00:19:43.270
Then they got it to Google Cloud
and they chopped it up further.

00:19:43.270 --> 00:19:45.020
They got 80 microservices.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:46.770
They were able to get
the agility that you

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:47.940
get with microservices.

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:49.470
But there's a problem, too.

00:19:49.470 --> 00:19:51.210
Because they went
from managing one

00:19:51.210 --> 00:19:54.630
monolith to going and
managing hundreds of services.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:58.530
And so in their set of
goals, getting a way

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:01.950
to go manage these
seamlessly, easily, uniformly

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:05.260
was much higher than going
and containerizing them first.

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:08.190
And so they said, we will go
and adopt the service mesh first

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:09.690
and then we will containerize.

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:11.250
And obviously, if
you think of it,

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:13.020
if you went and
containerized a monolith,

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:16.650
you probably wouldn't
get much, anyways.

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.390
So what was their blueprint?

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:19.860
What did they do?

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.950
The starting point was a
VM-based set of services.

00:20:22.950 --> 00:20:25.380
Obviously this is just
a very small subset

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:28.080
of what services they ran.

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:29.790
Then they added
the sidecar proxy

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:32.640
to their VM-based services.

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:34.260
They also deployed an ingress.

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:37.170
Now, ingress is a very
critical part of service mesh,

00:20:37.170 --> 00:20:39.360
because that is where you
are guarding the entry

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:40.680
point to your mesh.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.630
And you want it to
provide DDoS defense.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:44.850
You want it to be very
robust because that

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:48.060
is where a lot of your external
traffic comes and hits.

00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:50.220
And so they had an
ingress to provide

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:54.630
a secure and programmable
entry point to their mesh.

00:20:54.630 --> 00:20:58.710
And then they layered in the
service mesh control plane.

00:20:58.710 --> 00:21:02.100
And then they move some of their
services from VMs to containers

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.500
and they lived
happily ever after.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:07.880
At this point, you should
be asking me, but how?

00:21:07.880 --> 00:21:09.510
So let's take a
step back and let's

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:11.310
see how they actually did it.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:13.650
Now, every customer has
a strategy, generally,

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:17.010
when they do this type of
transition or transformation.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:19.650
And one of the strategies
that customer A had

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.657
was to go with managed
services wherever possible.

00:21:22.657 --> 00:21:24.240
And this was simply
so that they could

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:27.480
make the move faster without
having the expertise in-house

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:30.780
to go manage the infrastructure
or even build it.

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:33.270
So they had four
main considerations.

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:36.210
What do I go and use for
container management?

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:38.580
What do I do with the
service mesh control plane?

00:21:38.580 --> 00:21:40.980
Where do I get that
as a managed service?

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:42.960
Where do I get an
Envoy image from?

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:44.967
And then who manages
the Envoy lifecycle?

00:21:44.967 --> 00:21:46.800
Because that's one of
the frequent questions

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:49.360
we get, as well.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:51.750
So here is the
approach they took.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:53.550
Essentially for
container orchestration

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.520
they use GKE, which is
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:58.510
For the service mesh
traffic control plane,

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:00.510
they are planning to use
Traffic Director, which

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:02.610
just went beta yesterday.

00:22:02.610 --> 00:22:04.680
For the opinionated
and certified image,

00:22:04.680 --> 00:22:06.840
they used an image from Tetrate.

00:22:06.840 --> 00:22:08.940
And then for the Envoy
lifecycle management,

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:11.020
they plan to use a
Tetrate solution, as well,

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:13.730
and we'll talk about it shortly.

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:15.910
So I'm not going to
deep dive a lot into GKE

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:18.290
because there are several
excellent docs on it.

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:21.180
I highly recommend
watching them.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:22.640
But let's go to
Traffic Director.

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:25.100
So what exactly is
Traffic Director?

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:28.190
If you really think of
adopting the service mesh,

00:22:28.190 --> 00:22:30.770
one is the transformation
inside your organization

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:32.370
as Georgi spoke about.

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:34.130
And then the second
part of it is also

00:22:34.130 --> 00:22:37.100
having pieces in the control
plane that are delivered to you

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:38.540
as a service.

00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:40.700
And so that's why
yesterday in Urs's keynote

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:42.380
we launched Traffic Director.

00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:45.667
It's our new GCP-managed
service for the service mesh.

00:22:45.667 --> 00:22:47.750
And there's also detailed
talk on Traffic Director

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:49.890
from yesterday.

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.110
So here is what it does.

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:54.650
First of all, imagine
that you have your data

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:58.700
plane that's comprised of your
VM or container-based services.

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:01.478
You go and inject your
Envoy proxies in there

00:23:01.478 --> 00:23:03.020
and then the Traffic
Director becomes

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:04.120
the control plane for it.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:06.175
It will give you
global load balancing.

00:23:06.175 --> 00:23:07.550
It'll offload the
health checking

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:09.560
from your Envoy instances.

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:12.230
It'll also give you a
traffic driven auto scaling.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:15.240
It will also give you a bunch
of traffic control capabilities.

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:18.050
And if you already use Envoy
you're familiar with those--

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:21.390
canaries, traffic splitting,
traffic steering, metering,

00:23:21.390 --> 00:23:23.090
circuit breaking, and so on.

00:23:23.090 --> 00:23:25.550
And the nice part
is it's GCP managed.

00:23:25.550 --> 00:23:28.730
So you will get a production
grid as a layer [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:31.190
So it takes a lot of the toil
out of going and managing

00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:32.480
this stuff.

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:34.460
One of the key things
that we emphasized

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:38.480
when we built this out is it has
to work for VMs and containers

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:39.630
out of the box.

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:42.140
So if you actually look
at my example here,

00:23:42.140 --> 00:23:45.530
the first service is
essentially self managed Docker.

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:47.570
The second one is
a VM-based service.

00:23:47.570 --> 00:23:50.210
And then the third one is a
Kubernetes engine service.

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:52.520
So it is able to manage
all of these heterogeneous

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:55.980
environments very seamlessly.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:58.620
And then you can have things
like deploying your instances

00:23:58.620 --> 00:23:59.120
anywhere.

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:01.790
That's what you get out of
the global load balancer.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:03.210
And what that really means--

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:05.690
so why is global load
balancing important?

00:24:05.690 --> 00:24:08.430
The reason it is important
is that, first of all,

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:11.250
you don't need to configure
anything to make that happen.

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:14.450
All you do is create a
service, put your instances

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:17.440
in whichever part of GCP
regions that makes sense for you

00:24:17.440 --> 00:24:19.100
or where your users are.

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.020
And then you turn
on Traffic Director.

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:23.690
And what it will do is that if
your service in region-- let's

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:26.450
say all of the instances
for a service go down.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:28.220
When the traffic is
coming to that region,

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:31.700
it will seamlessly redirect it
or overflow to another region.

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:33.560
And you don't need
any configuration

00:24:33.560 --> 00:24:34.510
to make that happen.

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:36.770
That's implicitly built in
into the Traffic Director.

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:41.000
There's another very
interesting reason

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:43.100
I brought up Traffic
Director in this talk, which

00:24:43.100 --> 00:24:45.470
is how do you go
make the transition

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:48.240
from a VM-based
service to containers?

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:50.330
There is a construct
called network endpoint

00:24:50.330 --> 00:24:52.110
groups, which I'll talk about.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:55.220
So imagine initially your
service was sitting in the VM

00:24:55.220 --> 00:24:57.440
and we have what is called
managed instance group.

00:24:57.440 --> 00:24:59.810
That's where you specify
all your VM endpoints

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:01.630
and then your service
is essentially

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:03.470
instantiated into that.

00:25:03.470 --> 00:25:06.600
Now, imagine you wanted to
containerize that service.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:08.840
There's a construct called
network endpoint groups.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:11.840
And so you go create a
service, service 1 prime.

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:14.960
And you add all of the container
endpoints inside a network

00:25:14.960 --> 00:25:15.950
endpoint group.

00:25:15.950 --> 00:25:17.690
The network endpoint
groups lets you see

00:25:17.690 --> 00:25:19.080
those individual containers.

00:25:19.080 --> 00:25:20.810
And so you can load
balance to them.

00:25:20.810 --> 00:25:23.660
You can do traffic control
to them individually.

00:25:23.660 --> 00:25:25.820
And so now what you do
is you have a service--

00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:29.480
the same version of the service,
which is in VM instances,

00:25:29.480 --> 00:25:31.860
and another one which is
sitting in containers.

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:33.470
And so you sent traffic to both.

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:35.060
You have control
knobs so you can

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:39.360
say 99% goes here, 1% goes
here, and so on and so forth.

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:41.960
Once you do that, then
what you do is you

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:44.230
start draining your
VM based service.

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:46.640
So slowly, slowly, slowly,
you start shifting the traffic

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:47.942
to your containers.

00:25:47.942 --> 00:25:49.400
And then eventually
what you can do

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:51.530
is you can drain away
your VM-based service.

00:25:51.530 --> 00:25:53.730
And you can do all of
this without disruption,

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:56.510
which is why it's really huge
from the point of migrating

00:25:56.510 --> 00:26:00.560
a given service from
VMs to containers.

00:26:00.560 --> 00:26:02.040
And then this will
get even easier

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:05.280
because we have a plan to
support both a managed instance

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:08.412
group and a network endpoint
group behind the same service.

00:26:08.412 --> 00:26:10.620
And so you don't even need
to create another service.

00:26:10.620 --> 00:26:12.600
You just create a
network endpoint group

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.818
and then slowly
migrate your traffic

00:26:14.818 --> 00:26:17.110
from their managed instance
group to a network endpoint

00:26:17.110 --> 00:26:17.610
group.

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:19.440
So it just gives
you a very easy way

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:23.460
to go from VMs to containers
for a given service.

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:27.480
The second part of the problem
is like how do we go and manage

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:28.890
the Envoy lifecycle.

00:26:28.890 --> 00:26:31.440
This is a question
I get asked often.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.992
And I wanted to invite Dhi
from Tetrate to talk about some

00:26:33.992 --> 00:26:35.700
of the solutions they
have in this space.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:41.900
DHI AURRAHMAN: Hello, everyone.

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:43.050
Hi, everyone.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:44.930
I'm Dhi from Tetrate.

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:46.800
I'm a software
engineer at Tetrate.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:48.170
I'm very lucky to be here.

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:50.420
Thank you, Prajakta.

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:54.230
So the question is how we do
the Envoy lifecycle management.

00:26:54.230 --> 00:26:57.770
So the challenges with
Envoy lifecycle management

00:26:57.770 --> 00:27:00.860
will be where do we
get the Envoy binaries?

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:04.790
I mean, getting the trusted
and updated build of Envoy

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:05.660
is not easy.

00:27:05.660 --> 00:27:09.200
I mean, building yourselves
probably will be a torture.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:13.750
And then it would be another
one, integrated vulnerability

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:17.720
check and scanning,
and how if there

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:20.300
is a fix on upstream,
how you get it.

00:27:20.300 --> 00:27:24.110
And then how do you exactly do
it, that upgrading your Envoy?

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:30.260
So here at Tetrate, we
do this some tooling

00:27:30.260 --> 00:27:33.530
that do the initialization
and do the continuous stack

00:27:33.530 --> 00:27:36.620
in your infrastructure.

00:27:36.620 --> 00:27:40.100
And we do this out of the
attack, for every attack,

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:41.160
and so on and so forth.

00:27:41.160 --> 00:27:45.400
And we also offer
how to upgrade that.

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:49.090
So here, this is the GetEnvoy.

00:27:49.090 --> 00:27:54.190
GetEnvoy is one of our
products that we launched.

00:27:54.190 --> 00:27:59.680
It is still in preview,
but we will do this

00:27:59.680 --> 00:28:01.840
build distribution of Envoy.

00:28:01.840 --> 00:28:02.720
It is trusted.

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:04.840
It is certified.

00:28:04.840 --> 00:28:08.050
And there is there will
be a mechanism on how

00:28:08.050 --> 00:28:13.510
to upgrade Envoy and also how
we do the integrated scanning

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:17.300
for patching the Envoy.

00:28:17.300 --> 00:28:20.350
OK, so GetEnvoy by
Tetrate, Tetrate

00:28:20.350 --> 00:28:22.610
will open the access of
the build pipeline itself.

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:25.090
It will be there for public.

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:28.570
And we committed to
work with the community,

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:33.530
bringing tested Envoy build
with low costs in your site.

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:35.830
So we also work hard to
integrate that [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:35.830 --> 00:28:37.600
patching to upstream.

00:28:37.600 --> 00:28:42.790
So we also have additional
protocol support

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:43.630
if you need it.

00:28:43.630 --> 00:28:46.630
And we have full
attention support

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:48.970
from maintenance inside Tetrate.

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:53.590
So we have one senior maintainer
in Tetrate and for proxy

00:28:53.590 --> 00:28:56.410
and another maintainer.

00:28:56.410 --> 00:29:00.280
So this is a case when you
do the vulnerability scanning

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:04.090
synthetic attack to do
[INAUDIBLE] scanning.

00:29:04.090 --> 00:29:06.610
One of this is a
synthetic attack.

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:09.400
So basically, you
do checking on--

00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:13.030
so we have Envoy
built in our local--

00:29:13.030 --> 00:29:13.987
in your environment.

00:29:13.987 --> 00:29:15.820
And then we do the
synthetic attack on that.

00:29:15.820 --> 00:29:20.860
And we check if it
is vulnerable or not.

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:25.090
So this kind of a mechanism
will be integrated.

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:28.090
It can be standalone
it or it can be

00:29:28.090 --> 00:29:31.620
integrated to your environment.

00:29:31.620 --> 00:29:38.090
So more than that, we also
offer Envoy customization

00:29:38.090 --> 00:29:41.340
because probably you
need that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:29:41.340 --> 00:29:43.780
So this is--

00:29:43.780 --> 00:29:45.660
Envoy at its core is extensible.

00:29:45.660 --> 00:29:47.780
You can basically write
your very own extension.

00:29:47.780 --> 00:29:49.640
In fact, this
extensibility really

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:52.190
drives the adoption of Envoy.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:57.020
This is an invitation for
you to contribute to Envoy.

00:29:57.020 --> 00:30:00.410
You can check out Envoy
on how to stand Envoy.

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:07.810
So this is the
integration with--

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:12.040
well, when you get the
Envoy from someone else,

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:15.220
a third party like Tetrate,
probably you want to ask,

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:18.760
is it compatible with any
control plane out there?

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:23.200
So we do this integration
with Google's Traffic Director

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:27.190
and Apache SkyWalking
for observability.

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:32.025
So we have front end running in
a VM pointing to user service.

00:30:32.025 --> 00:30:33.400
At first, the user
service points

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:35.835
to profile service
that runs on VM.

00:30:35.835 --> 00:30:37.210
And then after
that, we gradually

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:41.380
program Traffic Director to
point to the profile service

00:30:41.380 --> 00:30:44.020
two on Kubernetes.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:45.700
So as Prajakta
[INAUDIBLE] to you

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:49.630
that we will drain
the traffic from VM

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:53.400
to Kubernetes by programming
the Traffic Director.

00:30:56.260 --> 00:30:59.110
So this is a video of the demo.

00:30:59.110 --> 00:31:04.140
So first we have two
different services running

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:09.550
deployed in Kubernetes and VM.

00:31:09.550 --> 00:31:15.790
And then we gradually apply the
rules to the Traffic Director

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:20.120
so we can move from
VM to Kubernetes.

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:25.420
So as you see that
here, once we implement

00:31:25.420 --> 00:31:33.550
the policy, the routing,
from VM to Kubernetes,

00:31:33.550 --> 00:31:35.480
we will see changes in the UI.

00:31:35.480 --> 00:31:38.150
So let's just wait on--

00:31:38.150 --> 00:31:42.580
OK, so as you see that when
we move the traffic from VM

00:31:42.580 --> 00:31:47.960
to Kubernetes, it would
show a fix on the UI.

00:31:47.960 --> 00:31:52.130
It's 50/50 traffic.

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:55.940
So I think that's all for me.

00:31:55.940 --> 00:31:59.320
I'll give it back to Prajakta.

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:00.300
Thank you.

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:03.730
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:06.528 --> 00:32:07.820
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Thank you, Dhi.

00:32:07.820 --> 00:32:09.930
And I think if you
didn't see the hearts,

00:32:09.930 --> 00:32:12.020
they were changing
between white and red.

00:32:12.020 --> 00:32:15.230
And so that was the 50/50
that was happening in there.

00:32:15.230 --> 00:32:18.260
So coming back to-- so
we spoke about customary,

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:20.940
and Dhi showed us also how
to go inject your Envoys.

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.015
And there's a bunch of
capabilities they provide.

00:32:23.015 --> 00:32:25.140
But then there's a different
type of customer, too.

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:27.890
So let's talk about
customer B. This

00:32:27.890 --> 00:32:30.200
is a customer based out of APAC.

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:32.880
And they had a
different set of goals,

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:36.770
which was let's containerize
first, and then we will mesh.

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:38.850
So how did they do it?

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:41.223
It seems like the common
theme is a monolith on prem.

00:32:41.223 --> 00:32:42.890
I need to add some
more blueprints where

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:44.640
that's not always the case.

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:48.680
But even this customer had
a monolith that sat on prem.

00:32:48.680 --> 00:32:51.818
And they said we need
to move to the cloud.

00:32:51.818 --> 00:32:53.360
While they move to
the cloud, we also

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:54.800
want to do some refactoring.

00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:57.470
And then we want to make sure
that as our services move

00:32:57.470 --> 00:32:59.750
to cloud, they are
more and more modern.

00:32:59.750 --> 00:33:01.520
And so that was their goal.

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:05.330
Now, what is the strategy
that they deployed?

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.070
The first thing that
they did actually

00:33:07.070 --> 00:33:10.790
is not much, in that they
left the monolith on prem.

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:13.400
Then they took Google's
global load balancer

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:16.800
and then they put a set of API
gateway instances behind that.

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:19.590
And as you can see, each of
these has a different purpose.

00:33:19.590 --> 00:33:22.430
So there's TLS termination,
DDoS defense that they got out

00:33:22.430 --> 00:33:24.110
of the global load balancer.

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:26.708
The API gateway obviously
did API-level routing.

00:33:26.708 --> 00:33:28.250
We have several
products like Apigee,

00:33:28.250 --> 00:33:29.870
as well, that you can use.

00:33:29.870 --> 00:33:32.390
Then you have, as
you notice, they

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:35.480
wanted to do everything in a
way that their services got

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.250
containerized as
they moved to cloud.

00:33:37.250 --> 00:33:39.770
So you can see everything
in Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:33:39.770 --> 00:33:41.940
Obviously, nothing
has moved there yet.

00:33:41.940 --> 00:33:44.002
And then you have
the monolith on prem.

00:33:44.002 --> 00:33:46.460
And simply the traffic is coming
in through the global load

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:48.650
balancer through
your API gateway

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:50.520
and getting routed to on prem.

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:54.050
So again, no disruption
till this point of time.

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:56.930
Next, what they did is they
took a chunk of a service out

00:33:56.930 --> 00:33:59.240
of the monolith-- you
can see service A--

00:33:59.240 --> 00:34:01.000
and then they put it on prem.

00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:02.750
When they moved it,
they containerized it.

00:34:02.750 --> 00:34:05.030
So when it was instantiated
in Google Cloud,

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:06.500
it was a containerized
service that

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:10.610
was managed with GKE or
Google Kubernetes engine.

00:34:10.610 --> 00:34:12.469
Similarly, they got
another service piece.

00:34:12.469 --> 00:34:14.761
So you can see that the
monolith has started to shrink,

00:34:14.761 --> 00:34:16.550
and then they got it
into Google Cloud.

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:19.453
That's your service B.

00:34:19.453 --> 00:34:20.870
And for many of
the services, they

00:34:20.870 --> 00:34:23.900
were able to rewrite the
services to use GRPC.

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:25.820
So in the event where
you can do rewrites,

00:34:25.820 --> 00:34:28.969
I would highly recommend looking
at GRPC as your transport

00:34:28.969 --> 00:34:30.159
interface.

00:34:30.159 --> 00:34:32.780
And when not, you can continue
to use your HTTP and so

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:35.250
on and so forth, as well.

00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:38.239
Now, once they did that,
there was some systems that--

00:34:38.239 --> 00:34:40.760
or some services that
would never move to GCP.

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:43.010
An example of this
was a payment service.

00:34:43.010 --> 00:34:44.510
And they shared
this payment service

00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:46.510
between various deployments,
so they didn't want

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:48.020
to move it to Google Cloud.

00:34:48.020 --> 00:34:51.389
But they still wanted all of the
modernization for that service.

00:34:51.389 --> 00:34:52.940
So what they did
is they chunked out

00:34:52.940 --> 00:34:54.860
that service, put
it on prem-- that's

00:34:54.860 --> 00:34:57.290
what you see what service
C. And then what they did

00:34:57.290 --> 00:34:58.910
is they wanted to
manage it using

00:34:58.910 --> 00:35:02.210
the same primitives as they
do with GKE and Google Cloud.

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:04.400
So they brought in GKE On Prem.

00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:07.130
And that is what they used
to manage this service now,

00:35:07.130 --> 00:35:08.633
which is sitting on prem.

00:35:08.633 --> 00:35:09.800
But the primitives are same.

00:35:09.800 --> 00:35:12.800
GKE and Google
Cloud, GKE On Prem,

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:15.290
and then they don't have to
relearn two new primitives

00:35:15.290 --> 00:35:17.328
in each case.

00:35:17.328 --> 00:35:19.370
Now, obviously, one service
sits in Google Cloud.

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:21.568
Another sits on prem.

00:35:21.568 --> 00:35:22.610
They have to communicate.

00:35:22.610 --> 00:35:25.100
So now you need service
to service communication.

00:35:25.100 --> 00:35:27.470
And that is what necessitated
a look at the service

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:30.020
mesh and then some of these
technologies about service

00:35:30.020 --> 00:35:31.920
to service communication.

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:34.730
So what they did is they first
injected the Envoy proxies--

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:37.070
you can see on service
B and service C.

00:35:37.070 --> 00:35:39.682
And then they manage
that with Istio on prem.

00:35:39.682 --> 00:35:41.390
And then they have an
Istio control plane

00:35:41.390 --> 00:35:44.660
running in GCP, as well.

00:35:44.660 --> 00:35:46.220
At this point of
time, most people

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:49.200
want to get out of running
the control planes themselves.

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:52.667
And so now with Traffic
Director in GCP site,

00:35:52.667 --> 00:35:54.500
they are going to manage
the traffic control

00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:56.300
plane using Traffic Director.

00:35:56.300 --> 00:35:57.800
And then for the
rest of the pieces,

00:35:57.800 --> 00:35:59.820
they'll continue
to leverage Istio.

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:02.930
And in the future, once
we support a hybrid model

00:36:02.930 --> 00:36:06.140
where Traffic Director can
manage instances on prem,

00:36:06.140 --> 00:36:08.510
then they will do the
same thing for the Istio.

00:36:08.510 --> 00:36:11.960
And then the Istio that is on
prem will remain as a fallback.

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:13.370
So in case Traffic
Director can't

00:36:13.370 --> 00:36:16.910
communicate with instances,
it will fall back.

00:36:16.910 --> 00:36:19.610
If you really think of it,
the next big innovation

00:36:19.610 --> 00:36:20.390
or the next big--

00:36:20.390 --> 00:36:21.682
I wouldn't call it a challenge.

00:36:21.682 --> 00:36:23.660
I think it's an opportunity
for innovation--

00:36:23.660 --> 00:36:26.360
is around federation of all
of these control planes.

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:29.340
And so you will see a lot of
conversations around this.

00:36:29.340 --> 00:36:32.030
But this is essentially the
thing that they deployed.

00:36:32.030 --> 00:36:33.920
And so if we had to
summarize the steps,

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.460
this is what it looks like.

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.540
So they went from a monolith--
and if you just go 1, 2, 3, 4,

00:36:38.540 --> 00:36:40.730
5, 6, those are
the steps that they

00:36:40.730 --> 00:36:45.620
took to get to a
modern architecture.

00:36:45.620 --> 00:36:47.110
So in all of this,
I know we spoke

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:48.760
about a bunch of solutions.

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:52.960
We had Georgi describe
how Walmart does it.

00:36:52.960 --> 00:36:56.150
They describe some of the
solutions from Tetrate.

00:36:56.150 --> 00:36:58.930
We also have a bunch of
solutions in Google Cloud.

00:36:58.930 --> 00:37:06.350
And one of the questions I
often get asked by customers is,

00:37:06.350 --> 00:37:08.330
should we use Google
Cloud products?

00:37:08.330 --> 00:37:11.520
Because they are best of breed,
sure, but do they lock us in?

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:14.060
And it's a very frequent
question that I get.

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:15.950
And my question
always back to them

00:37:15.950 --> 00:37:17.360
is, what if you could get both?

00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:20.360
You get best-of-breed products,
but there's also no lock in.

00:37:20.360 --> 00:37:22.010
And the key to
making that happen

00:37:22.010 --> 00:37:24.660
is essentially the
open interfaces.

00:37:24.660 --> 00:37:27.000
So we'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:37:27.000 --> 00:37:28.790
But the first thing--
so let's take a look

00:37:28.790 --> 00:37:29.912
at the migration tools.

00:37:29.912 --> 00:37:32.120
Here, there is no lock in,
because you're essentially

00:37:32.120 --> 00:37:35.570
using these to do possibly
a one time migration.

00:37:35.570 --> 00:37:37.740
I don't know how many of
you attended the keynote,

00:37:37.740 --> 00:37:40.220
but if you did, you saw the
announcement of a product

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:41.870
called Anthos Migrate.

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:43.490
And then it's at beta.

00:37:43.490 --> 00:37:46.910
It auto migrates VMs
from on-prem into GKE.

00:37:46.910 --> 00:37:50.480
And so that is very minimal
effort on your end to do it.

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:52.680
There's an excellent
talk on it, as well.

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:55.040
And then what that means
is when you use-- and it's

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:57.320
very holistic in its approach.

00:37:57.320 --> 00:37:59.930
So you can see it does
discovery, migration planning.

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:01.820
There's a whole set
of steps that it

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:05.930
does to get your VMs
into GKE in Google Cloud.

00:38:05.930 --> 00:38:08.210
And so what that means is
your own folks can actually

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:11.120
take their time and focus on
managing your applications

00:38:11.120 --> 00:38:13.940
without really having to worry
about how to do the migration

00:38:13.940 --> 00:38:16.590
and so on and so forth.

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:19.700
Now, once you've migrated--
or even when you're in GCP--

00:38:19.700 --> 00:38:22.520
the question is, what are
the tools you should use

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:24.720
and where should you
worry about lock in?

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:28.812
So the answer is, wherever you
find an open interface, that

00:38:28.812 --> 00:38:30.770
is where you don't have
to worry about lock-in,

00:38:30.770 --> 00:38:32.390
because if you don't
like something,

00:38:32.390 --> 00:38:34.910
you can swap it out as long
as the interface is open.

00:38:34.910 --> 00:38:37.320
And I'll give you an example.

00:38:37.320 --> 00:38:40.060
Let's say as a starting point,
you are our load balancing

00:38:40.060 --> 00:38:40.560
customers.

00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:42.435
You don't really care
about the service mesh.

00:38:42.435 --> 00:38:44.810
You don't really care about
Traffic Director initially.

00:38:44.810 --> 00:38:47.510
So you're looking for
another flavor of L7 or LB,

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:49.050
or something like that.

00:38:49.050 --> 00:38:52.340
And then what you do is imagine
that you suddenly now said,

00:38:52.340 --> 00:38:54.995
I want to go and modernize
my load balancing.

00:38:54.995 --> 00:38:56.870
And so I want to go look
at the service mesh.

00:38:56.870 --> 00:38:59.210
So there is a huge bunch
of customers who start off

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:01.890
as load balancing
customers and then go on

00:39:01.890 --> 00:39:03.892
to become service
mesh customers.

00:39:03.892 --> 00:39:06.350
And in there, one of the nice
things about Traffic Director

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:08.480
is when it speaks
to the Envoy, it

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:11.870
uses XDS APIs, which is
what the open interface used

00:39:11.870 --> 00:39:16.340
for any service mesh control
plane that talks to Envoys.

00:39:16.340 --> 00:39:19.790
And so if you don't like Traffic
Director, you can swap it out.

00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:22.550
And it doesn't even have to
be Envoy in your data plane,

00:39:22.550 --> 00:39:26.090
because as long as that
proxy supports XDS,

00:39:26.090 --> 00:39:27.570
you could bring your own proxy.

00:39:27.570 --> 00:39:30.350
You could go customize
Envoy, as long as you

00:39:30.350 --> 00:39:31.730
preserve that open interface.

00:39:31.730 --> 00:39:33.770
And so that's the
track we are taking

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:36.960
to ensure that there's no lock
in, while at the same time,

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:39.450
we do want to take away the
toil of managing these control

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:41.760
planes.

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:44.630
So actually, there's
another very interesting set

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:48.500
of customers who are like, I
want all of this modernization

00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:51.650
without having to worry about
Traffic Director or Envoy.

00:39:51.650 --> 00:39:52.870
And they're like, OK.

00:39:52.870 --> 00:39:55.700
We have a service setting
which just cannot--

00:39:55.700 --> 00:39:57.510
I cannot go inject
an Envoy in there.

00:39:57.510 --> 00:39:59.640
So what do I go to there?

00:39:59.640 --> 00:40:03.170
And so that is why we created
another flavor called L7 ILB.

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:06.050
So it looks like any other
load balancer to you.

00:40:06.050 --> 00:40:09.080
And so what you would do is you
would get all of the benefits

00:40:09.080 --> 00:40:11.600
that you get from traffic
control and other primitives

00:40:11.600 --> 00:40:15.170
from [INAUDIBLE] Envoy,
but you don't necessarily

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:17.570
need to even worry about
a control plane or a data

00:40:17.570 --> 00:40:18.770
plane or running Envoys.

00:40:18.770 --> 00:40:21.350
It just looks like any other
[INAUDIBLE] proxy to you.

00:40:21.350 --> 00:40:23.990
And you take it and you put
it in front of your services.

00:40:23.990 --> 00:40:26.690
What it really looks like
under the hood is this.

00:40:26.690 --> 00:40:30.050
So it is Traffic Director which
is managing a pool of Envoys

00:40:30.050 --> 00:40:32.540
for you, but it's
abstracting all of this out

00:40:32.540 --> 00:40:35.420
and just making it look like
any [INAUDIBLE] proxy to you.

00:40:35.420 --> 00:40:38.300
So it's which
technologies you adopt

00:40:38.300 --> 00:40:40.910
and which sequence is a
function of the skill set

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:44.602
that you have in your folks,
or what your goals are,

00:40:44.602 --> 00:40:46.310
or what is the strategy
you want to start

00:40:46.310 --> 00:40:48.852
with, what other considerations
do you have in terms of cost,

00:40:48.852 --> 00:40:50.160
time, and so on?

00:40:50.160 --> 00:40:51.920
And once you've
nailed that down,

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:53.930
then it gets easier to
start picking one or more

00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:56.710
of these technologies.

00:40:56.710 --> 00:40:59.020
There's another set of
customers who actually is not

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:00.760
from the load balancing pool.

00:41:00.760 --> 00:41:03.460
Now, they come in from the
point of view of saying,

00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:05.140
I have these multi
cloud services,

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:06.720
or I have microservices.

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:09.040
And I want to go and
consistently and uniformly

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:10.090
manage them.

00:41:10.090 --> 00:41:12.820
And so they are probably
already GKE customers,

00:41:12.820 --> 00:41:14.920
or they may be looking at GKE.

00:41:14.920 --> 00:41:17.050
Several of these
might have used Envoy

00:41:17.050 --> 00:41:18.790
in various forms and shapes.

00:41:18.790 --> 00:41:21.430
Many of them may be open
source to your users.

00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:22.990
And so that is where,
again, you have

00:41:22.990 --> 00:41:25.900
a whole slew of options in
here, ranging from open source

00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:27.070
to Google Manage.

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:28.585
And the key in
each of these-- so

00:41:28.585 --> 00:41:33.010
like whether you self manage
Kubernetes or whether use a GKE

00:41:33.010 --> 00:41:35.020
or whether you use
GKE on prem, it's

00:41:35.020 --> 00:41:37.120
the same set of APIs
and primitives, which

00:41:37.120 --> 00:41:38.480
means you don't get locked in.

00:41:38.480 --> 00:41:40.480
You can switch
between each of these.

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:42.490
Similarly, if you
use Traffic Director

00:41:42.490 --> 00:41:44.730
or you use Istio
to talk to Envoy,

00:41:44.730 --> 00:41:48.100
or you have your home grown
control plane that talks xDSv2,

00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:50.500
you don't get locked in,
because you have the capability

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:53.260
to go and switch, because
the APIs and the interfaces

00:41:53.260 --> 00:41:54.123
are open.

00:41:54.123 --> 00:41:55.540
And then there's
another one which

00:41:55.540 --> 00:41:58.420
I think we don't talk about
enough, which is GRPC.

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:01.420
If you have services
that are using HTTP, HTTP

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:06.500
1.1, et cetera, and you want to
bring in HTTP 2 as an example,

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:08.140
then you would--
you bring in HTTP 2,

00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:10.195
you would bring in
GRPC, and so on.

00:42:10.195 --> 00:42:12.070
If you have services
that you cannot rewrite,

00:42:12.070 --> 00:42:14.630
then you can keep it as a goal
and save it up for your phase

00:42:14.630 --> 00:42:17.310
2.

00:42:17.310 --> 00:42:19.140
Then the next thing
which I often get

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:20.290
asked-- so all of these.

00:42:20.290 --> 00:42:22.350
But there is a set
of people who don't

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:24.832
want to see any infrastructure
at all, whether it's

00:42:24.832 --> 00:42:26.790
service management
infrastructure, whether it's

00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:28.930
container orchestration,
and so on.

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:31.920
And that is where, really, if
you were there yesterday or day

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:34.060
before, you heard
about Cloud Run,

00:42:34.060 --> 00:42:35.310
that's essentially serverless.

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:37.110
And again, the same question.

00:42:37.110 --> 00:42:39.090
Like, do we get locked
into your solutions?

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:41.490
And the answer is no,
because if you look at it,

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:43.020
first there's Cloud Run.

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:44.070
So it's fully managed.

00:42:44.070 --> 00:42:45.300
You deploy your workloads.

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:46.510
You don't see the cluster.

00:42:46.510 --> 00:42:48.640
It's fully managed
service for you.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:50.250
But then I have a GKE cluster.

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:52.715
So then you go and
use Cloud Run on GKE.

00:42:52.715 --> 00:42:54.090
And then what that
lets you do is

00:42:54.090 --> 00:42:56.215
you run the serverless side
by side your container,

00:42:56.215 --> 00:42:58.030
so your existing workloads.

00:42:58.030 --> 00:42:59.580
And then it runs seamlessly.

00:42:59.580 --> 00:43:02.310
But then a lot of you also have
on prem or other deployments

00:43:02.310 --> 00:43:03.390
of Kubernetes.

00:43:03.390 --> 00:43:05.760
And then you are like,
what about Cloud Run there?

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:07.825
And so that's what
Knative lets you do.

00:43:07.825 --> 00:43:10.200
I don't know how many of you
attended all of these talks.

00:43:10.200 --> 00:43:13.140
But they're excellent talks that
go through these three options.

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:14.640
And the key is, again, the same.

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:18.480
You're using the same tooling,
the same APIs across the board

00:43:18.480 --> 00:43:20.610
and wherever you're
on Kubernetes.

00:43:20.610 --> 00:43:22.110
And then you get
the same primitives

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:23.830
without having to relearn.

00:43:23.830 --> 00:43:25.650
And I think that is
the broader theme

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:28.110
I want you to take away from
a lot of these services that

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:29.230
are being built.

00:43:29.230 --> 00:43:32.340
So when we say we build
tools for multi cloud,

00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:34.590
a very key aspect
of it is it has

00:43:34.590 --> 00:43:38.640
to support these open
interfaces, which

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:39.840
brings me back to the end.

00:43:39.840 --> 00:43:42.070
So we spoke about
Walmart's journey.

00:43:42.070 --> 00:43:44.790
We spoke about customer A,
who first meshed and then

00:43:44.790 --> 00:43:46.050
containerized.

00:43:46.050 --> 00:43:47.640
And then we spoke
about customer B

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:50.168
who actually first
containerized and then meshed.

00:43:50.168 --> 00:43:52.710
And imagine that there are three
or more other patterns where

00:43:52.710 --> 00:43:55.350
they're not necessarily
starting from monoliths.

00:43:55.350 --> 00:43:58.230
In fact, we have
a talk today where

00:43:58.230 --> 00:44:00.660
we talk about how
service measure's equally

00:44:00.660 --> 00:44:02.860
applicable to Telco workloads.

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:05.370
So one of the key things
about these technologies

00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:07.680
is, they don't really
make assumptions

00:44:07.680 --> 00:44:10.830
about whether it's VMs,
containers, whether it

00:44:10.830 --> 00:44:12.580
runs in Google or elsewhere.

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:14.370
Like, these are very
good abstractions

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:16.060
from that point of view.

00:44:16.060 --> 00:44:18.660
And so how to use them and
when to use them actually

00:44:18.660 --> 00:44:20.920
boils down to having a plan.

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:22.500
And here are the
typical steps that

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:25.420
are common across these
five or six blueprints.

00:44:25.420 --> 00:44:27.960
So when you start your
journey to going and migrating

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:29.850
your own workloads,
or even bringing

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:32.160
all of these technologies
to brownfield,

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:33.780
you have to start
with your goals.

00:44:33.780 --> 00:44:35.430
Like, what are your goals?

00:44:35.430 --> 00:44:36.930
Why is it that you
want to bring in?

00:44:36.930 --> 00:44:39.480
And hopefully the answer is
not just because they are cool,

00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:42.060
because all of these are
extremely cool technologies.

00:44:42.060 --> 00:44:43.840
But there has to be
some business goal

00:44:43.840 --> 00:44:47.010
or there has to be something
around agility or some problem

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:48.420
that it solves for you.

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:50.590
And that is the crux
of it, honestly.

00:44:50.590 --> 00:44:52.890
Once you've identified
that, then you

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:54.660
can map to one of
these strategies.

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:56.040
We'll have a lot
more papers that

00:44:56.040 --> 00:44:59.250
go out that describe each
of these in detail and more.

00:44:59.250 --> 00:45:01.300
And so once you've
identified your strategy,

00:45:01.300 --> 00:45:03.910
then the strategy will
let you choose tools.

00:45:03.910 --> 00:45:06.600
And that will depend on what
is the kind of tool you want?

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:09.750
Like, I would say the table
stakes is open interfaces.

00:45:09.750 --> 00:45:12.210
Like, look for open
interfaces because then you

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:13.240
can change your mind.

00:45:13.240 --> 00:45:15.720
You can swap things out
and bring in new tools.

00:45:15.720 --> 00:45:17.370
And this could be
Google Cloud tools.

00:45:17.370 --> 00:45:19.050
These could be
third party tools.

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:21.060
Often, we have a lot
of complementary tools

00:45:21.060 --> 00:45:23.400
for the problems that you
face and we don't necessarily

00:45:23.400 --> 00:45:24.700
have a solution.

00:45:24.700 --> 00:45:27.250
So identify your
services and tools.

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:29.490
Make sure you've done your
due diligence on them.

00:45:29.490 --> 00:45:33.300
Once you've done that, most of
the success stories that we see

00:45:33.300 --> 00:45:36.510
are customers who have
had an MVP use case, which

00:45:36.510 --> 00:45:38.490
means that there's
a set of services

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:41.430
they identified and then
they execute the strategy

00:45:41.430 --> 00:45:42.930
with those small
set of services.

00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:45.270
And then once they
see success, they

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:49.525
will go and optimize,
because obviously there

00:45:49.525 --> 00:45:52.210
are learnings from their
first phase of execution.

00:45:52.210 --> 00:45:54.330
And then they'll optimize
and it's rinse repeat.

00:45:54.330 --> 00:45:57.690
And in all of these,
one of the critical bits

00:45:57.690 --> 00:45:59.950
is essentially to automate,
automate, automate.

00:45:59.950 --> 00:46:01.210
I can't stress that enough.

00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:03.930
But again, the success
stories of making these things

00:46:03.930 --> 00:46:06.120
repeatable, easy to
manage, is that you

00:46:06.120 --> 00:46:08.250
bring in the CI/CD pipelines.

00:46:08.250 --> 00:46:10.470
We have customers who manage--

00:46:10.470 --> 00:46:12.360
like, code in CI/CD pipelines.

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:15.520
And it just makes their
lives that much easier.

00:46:15.520 --> 00:46:17.590
So I know it was a bit
of a whirlwind tour,

00:46:17.590 --> 00:46:20.010
but we hope we gave
you at least a flavor

00:46:20.010 --> 00:46:21.480
of the various
strategies you can

00:46:21.480 --> 00:46:24.330
use to bring in service
mesh, containers,

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:26.610
and modern technologies
into your brownfield.

00:46:26.610 --> 00:46:28.110
So with that, thank you.

00:46:28.110 --> 00:46:31.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

